goals.en:
After completing the course “Climate Data and Services”, students must be able to: 
-	Describe the main methods of climate data production (e.g., ground-based measurements, satellite-derived data, and climate models);
-	Recognize the limitations and main uncertainty sources associated with climate data;
-	Describe the main drivers of the climate system and the main sources of climate variability;
-	Assess changes in climate at local, regional and global scale; 
-	Recognize the challenges to climate change adaptation posed by climate extremes (e.g., heatwaves, droughts, wildfires, major storms, glacial lake outburst floods (GLOF), and harmful algal blooms (HABs);
-	Make use of open access climate datasets produced and maintained by well-known providers of climate services (e.g., NASA, NOAA, NSIDC, ESA, Copernicus, and KNMI); 
-	Support smart decision making and climate adaptation efforts by gathering and making sense of climate data at local, regional, and global scale.
